  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 155 PM CDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\n     TONIGHT THROUGH MONDAY NIGHT...\n\nUpper air analysis this afternoon shows a weak upper ridge beginning \nto build in out over the Rocky Mountains and into the \nsouthern/central plains. At the surface, the surface ridge that \naided in bringing the region's first frost/freeze of the season\nearlier this morning has translated eastward, and light \nsoutherly low-level flow has returned. Still, afternoon \ntemperatures will be a bit cooler than average for this time of \nyear with highs topping out in the upper 50s to low 60s. \nTonight, light winds and clear skies will support ample \nradiational cooling, and another night/early morning with \ntemperatures in the 30s is forecast. While temperatures should \nremain above freezing, some frost is possible, mainly across \nportions of south-central and southeast Kansas. Breezy southerly\nwinds throughout the day on Sunday should support afternoon \nhighs in the 60s, and continued light southerly flow will keep \novernight lows Sunday night in the 40s. Similar conditions are \nexpected on Monday with breezy southerly winds, afternoon highs \nin the mid to upper 60s, and lows in the 40s.\n\n     TUESDAY THROUGH NEXT WEEKEND...\n\nDeterministic and ensemble guidance is beginning to show a subtle \nmid/upper trough racing northeast out New Mexico and into the \ncentral plains. Limited moisture should prevent the development of \nwidespread showers, but there should be enough synoptic ascent for \ncloud cover and perhaps a few sprinkles on Tuesday. Persistent \nsoutherly low level flow will continue advecting warmer \ntemperatures into the region with afternoon highs likely to rise\ninto the 70s on Tuesday despite the increased cloud cover. \nPersistent deep layer troughing across the western CONUS and \nmid/upper ridging across the central plains should continue to \nencourage a marked warming trend from mid to late next week. \nThis upper pattern should keep southwesterly upper flow across \nthe southern Rockies promoting gradual surface pressure falls \nacross the Front Range and High Plains supporting continued \nsoutherly low-level flow. As a result, highs Wednesday through \nSaturday are generally forecast to be in the upper 70s to low \n80s each afternoon. This is slightly lower than in previous \nforecasts as mid/long range guidance shows an increased \nlikelihood for cloud cover in response to broad, weak synoptic \nascent overspreading the central plains. However, sufficient \nmoisture for precipitation looks to hold off until late next \nweekend or early next week. Long range global models remain a \nbit all over the place, but most show another Eastern Pacific \ntropical cyclone developing off the coast of Mexico, eventually \nrecurving with moisture from its remnants streaming northward \ninto the southern and central plains by this weekend or next\nweek. Details still remain incredibly fuzzy this far out, and \ntrends will need to monitored over the next week or so.",
